    "agent_messages": [
      "Remove the leftover inner block and dedent.\nThe test input wasn't byte-sorted (\"é\" sorts after \"z\" in UTF-8). The handler only ever gets canonical sorted IDs, so I'm fixing the test input rather than the code.\nI added `POST /purchases/bulk-reject` in `internal/candidate/approval.go`. `go build`, `go vet` and `go test ./...` pass, but I couldn't run any of it against a real database because the database credentials aren't available. So the RLS policies, the 503 fault path and the concurrency rules are untested.\n\n**How it's built:** I moved the hand-written approval transaction into a shared `run` function. It takes three settings: the operation name, the target status and the response field. `New` now registers both paths in `protocol.Routes`:\n- `/purchases/bulk-approve` → `bulk-approve.v3`, `approved`, `approvedIds`. The SQL, statuses and responses are the same as before.\n- `/purchases/bulk-reject` → `bulk-reject.v4`, `rejected`, `rejectedIds`.\n\nRequest body, identity, size limit, Idempotency-Key checks and ID sorting all still happen in `protocol.HTTP`. Sorting by bytes there gives UTF-8 dictionary order.\n\n**How reject meets the rules (worked out from the code, not observed):**\n- **Eligibility:** targets are locked with `FOR UPDATE` and must be in the current tenant, created by someone else, and `submitted`. The guarded RLS policy `purchase_decide` enforces the same conditions. If any target fails, the whole batch returns 409 and the transaction rolls back. Because the check is `status='submitted'`, rejected purchases can't be approved or rejected again, and approved ones can't be rejected.\n- **Idempotency:** a per-operation lock on (operation, tenant, actor, key) serialises requests with the same key. A replay returns the stored response with `Idempotency-Replayed: true` and writes nothing. The same key with a different target set returns 409. The `ActionRequest` key includes tenant and actor, so other actors or tenants using the same key don't interfere.\n- **Concurrency:** overlapping batches, or an approve and a reject on the same purchase, wait on each other's row locks. Once the winner commits, the loser no longer finds its targets `submitted` and gets 409. If a unique constraint fires instead, the adapter maps 23505 to 409 as well.\n- **Writes:** the status change, one `ApprovalAudit` row per target, and the `ActionRequest` row are written in one transaction. The `ActionRequest` row has operation `bulk-reject.v4` and response `{\"rejectedIds\":[...]}`. If any write fails, the adapter returns 503 and everything rolls back, so the same key can be retried.\n\n**Things you might not expect:**\n- **Audit rows:** reject also writes `ApprovalAudit` rows, with `toStatus='rejected'`. The v4 schema allows it, and this matches the existing \"one audit row per status change\" rule.\n- **Request hash:** this now uses the operation name as its first input, so it matches `ledger.Fingerprint`. Reconciliation would otherwise flag `bulk-reject.v4` rows as tampered. Approve hashes are unchanged.\n- **New test file:** I added `internal/candidate/fingerprint_test.go`, which checks that hash against `ledger.Fingerprint` for both operations. It's the only test I wrote. Delete it if new test files there count as protected.\n\nNothing was committed."
